    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the range of the function \\( G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1| \\), we need to consider the behavior of the function in different intervals determined by the points where the expressions inside the absolute values change sign. These points are \\( x = -1 \\) and \\( x = 1 \\). So, we will consider three cases: \\( x < -1 \\), \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\), and \\( x \\geq 1 \\).\n\n**Case 1: \\( x < -1 \\)**\nIn this interval, both \\( x+1 \\) and \\( x-1 \\) are negative. Therefore, we have:\n\\[ |x+1| = -(x+1) = -x-1 \\]\n\\[ |x-1| = -(x-1) = -x+1 \\]\nSo, the function becomes:\n\\[ G(x) = (-x-1) - (-x+1) = -x-1 + x-1 = -2 \\]\nThus, for \\( x < -1 \\), the value of \\( G(x) \\) is always \\(-2\\).\n\n**Case 2: \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\)**\nIn this interval, \\( x+1 \\) is non-negative and \\( x-1 \\) is negative. Therefore, we have:\n\\[ |x+1| = x+1 \\]\n\\[ |x-1| = -(x-1) = -x+1 \\]\nSo, the function becomes:\n\\[ G(x) = (x+1) - (-x+1) = x+1 + x-1 = 2x \\]\nThus, for \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\), the function \\( G(x) = 2x \\) varies from \\(-2\\) to \\(2\\).\n\n**Case 3: \\( x \\geq 1 \\)**\nIn this interval, both \\( x+1 \\) and \\( x-1 \\) are non-negative. Therefore, we have:\n\\[ |x+1| = x+1 \\]\n\\[ |x-1| = x-1 \\]\nSo, the function becomes:\n\\[ G(x) = (x+1) - (x-1) = x+1 - x+1 = 2 \\]\nThus, for \\( x \\geq 1 \\), the value of \\( G(x) \\) is always \\(2\\).\n\nCombining the results from all three cases, we see that the function \\( G(x) \\) takes on all values from \\(-2\\) to \\(2\\) (inclusive). Therefore, the range of the function \\( G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1| \\) is \\([-2, 2]\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{[-2, 2]}\n\\]",
